  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 227 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Broad high pressure remains firmly in place across \nsouthern Quebec/Ontario, spreading over the international border and \ninto northern New York/New England. This feature shows no sign of \nbudging over the next few days, and confidence has increased that we \nnow will stay dry through at least Friday. Mostly clear skies will \npersist through Thursday, along with light winds. Temperatures will \nremain seasonable, with highs mainly in the 60s and lows in the 30s \nto near 40F. There will be another round of frost away from Lake \nChamplain tonight, and expect subsequent shifts will issue another \nFrost Advisory for tonight. \n\nClouds start to increase Thursday night and Friday as low pressure \noff the Mid Atlantic coast attempts to force its way north toward \ncoastal New England. Model guidance has come into better agreement \nin keeping any precipitation associated with this system shunted to \nour south through Friday, so no rain is expected in spite of the \nincreasing cloud cover. Thursday will be a little warmer than the \nprevious couple of days, with highs in the mid 60s to around 70F.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Models continue to exhibit differing solutions in how a \ncoastal system will evolve as it meanders off the New England coast \nthis weekend. Just how close to the coast it tracks is still \nuncertain, as is how far north and west its precipitation spreads \ninland. Given the strength of the high to our north and the extent \nof the dry air, it seems likely there will be a tight precipitation \ngradient on the northern/western side of the system, so small \nchanges in track could result in significant changes in rain chances \nand amounts. There will also be a tightening pressure gradient over \nour area between the strengthening low and the high pressure \nanchored over southern Canada, so at the least expect winds to \nincrease late this week and over the weekend, particularly over the \nhigher terrain and down the Champlain Valley, where gusts of 25 to \n35 mph look possible, likely higher at summit level. \n\nOne other potential impact from this system will be its effect on \ntemperatures. With the very dry air currently in place, any rain \nthat does hold together long enough to wrap back into our region \nwould likely result in sharply cooler conditions; note that \ndeterministic guidance lowers temperatures into the 50s where rain \nwould be falling.",
